The three localized case properties.

localizedUppercaseString, localizedLowercaseString and localizedCapitalizedString, 10.11 and later,
were absent entirely: the header carried the three plain forms and the three WithLocale: forms and
none of these, so every caller took an unrecognized selector. iTerm2 lost its whole Preferences nib
decode to one of them, silently, because the raise is caught during a decode.

Each is the WithLocale: form taken against currentLocale, which is the documented difference and
the one that matters for Turkish dotless i and German sharp s.

HELD BACK DELIBERATELY FOR SEVERAL ROUNDS, and the reason belongs with them. Supplying these let
iTerm2 finish its Preferences decode, and finishing walked it into a SIGSEGV that had always been
there and was unreachable: TISCreateInputSourceList was a compat placeholder being CALLED as a
function, and once that was implemented, an input source that could not say what it was took
CFStringCompare into a NULL. Both are fixed. Landing these first would have turned a window that
does not open into an application that dies and takes the display with it.

The methods were never in doubt. tests/foundation/probe_localized_case.m runs all three WithLocale:
conversions against a real __NSCFLocale and passes 5 of 5, with the nil form as a control in every
case.

Verified on Command comma against iTerm2 3.5.14 with launchd on: UNRECOGNIZED 0, signal 11 count 0,
capture CONTENT. Preferences still does not open, which is a separate thread.

COST NOTE: declaring these in NSString.h rebuilds about 5900 actions, as far out as JavaScriptCore.
While bisecting, put a method in the .m alone, which rebuilds 4 and behaves identically because an
unrecognized selector is a runtime send.

--- a/src/NSString.m
+++ b/src/NSString.m
@@ -1616,6 +1616,27 @@
     return [self capitalizedStringWithLocale:nil];
 }
 
+/*
+ * THE LOCALE AWARE TRIO, 10.11 and later: the plain conversions taken against the CURRENT locale
+ * rather than none, which is the difference that matters for Turkish dotless i and German sharp s.
+ * Absent entirely, so any caller took an unrecognized selector; iTerm2 lost its whole Preferences
+ * nib decode to one. See docs/iterm2-preferences-gap.md.
+ */
+- (NSString *)localizedUppercaseString
+{
+    return [self uppercaseStringWithLocale:[NSLocale currentLocale]];
+}
+
+- (NSString *)localizedLowercaseString
+{
+    return [self lowercaseStringWithLocale:[NSLocale currentLocale]];
+}
+
+- (NSString *)localizedCapitalizedString
+{
+    return [self capitalizedStringWithLocale:[NSLocale currentLocale]];
+}
+
 - (NSString *)stringByTrimmingCharactersInSet:(NSCharacterSet *)set
 {
     if (set == nil)
--- a/include/Foundation/NSString.h
+++ b/include/Foundation/NSString.h
@@ -168,6 +168,10 @@
 - (NSString *)uppercaseStringWithLocale:(NSLocale *)locale;
 - (NSString *)lowercaseStringWithLocale:(NSLocale *)locale;
 - (NSString *)capitalizedStringWithLocale:(NSLocale *)locale;
+/* The same three against the CURRENT locale, 10.11 and later. */
+- (NSString *)localizedUppercaseString;
+- (NSString *)localizedLowercaseString;
+- (NSString *)localizedCapitalizedString;
 - (NSString *)stringByTrimmingCharactersInSet:(NSCharacterSet *)set;
